title: The Theses
subtitle: The Zentrale prepares for the congress.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1919 and month = 9 and crisis = 0
max-visits: 1
priority: 2
on-arrival: {!
// between the great clashes, the state of siege eases a little
Q.repression -= 6;
// the Left Radicals expect to win the coming congress, so they do not walk out before it
if (!Q.ultraleft_gone && Q.heidelberg_outcome == 0) { Q.ultraleft_satisfaction = Math.max(Q.ultraleft_satisfaction, 30); }
Q.militancy -= 2;
var g = Math.max(1.05, Math.min(1.35, 1.24 + Q.worker_support / 200 - Q.repression / 400));
for (var d of Q.districts) {
    Q['district_' + d + '_members'] *= g;
}
var total = 0;
for (var d of Q.districts) {
    total += Q['district_' + d + '_members'];
}
Q.members_display = total.toFixed(1);
!}

= The Theses

The party now has [+ members_display +] thousand members, most of them new, most of them young, many of them unemployed. The second party congress is called for October, in secret, somewhere in Baden. [+ party_chair +] has drafted theses on communist principles and tactics: the party must use elections and parliament as a platform; it must work inside the trade unions, where the workers are; it must be a centralised party, not a federation of districts. Anyone who rejects these principles, the last thesis says, stands outside the party.

The Left Radicals know that the last thesis is about them. They hold Hamburg, Bremen and much of Berlin, and they say that a congress of delegates elected by the members would vote the theses down. The Zentrale must decide how the delegates are chosen.

- @zentrale: The Zentrale sets the rules for the mandates.
- @districts: One delegate per district, whatever its size.
- @free: Free elections, one delegate per thousand members.

@zentrale
subtitle: Levi's way: a congress the Zentrale can rely on. The Left Radicals will call it rigged.
on-arrival: heid_mandates = 1; unity -= 5; ultraleft_satisfaction -= 10; luxemburgist_satisfaction += 5; lean_luxemburgist += 2; lean_ultraleft -= 1

Mandates go to district committees that the Zentrale has checked, and the members of the Zentrale vote too. In Hamburg and Berlin, the Left Radicals talk of a packed congress before it has even met.

@districts
subtitle: The Right's compromise: the small loyal districts count as much as the big rebellious ones.
on-arrival: heid_mandates = 2; unity -= 2; ultraleft_satisfaction -= 5; unionist_satisfaction += 4; lean_unionist += 1; lean_luxemburgist += 1

Thuringia and Halle get as many delegates as Hamburg. The rule can be defended in public. Everyone knows why it was chosen.

@free
subtitle: Party democracy, as Luxemburg wanted it. The Left Radicals may well win.
on-arrival: heid_mandates = 0; unity += 4; ultraleft_satisfaction += 6; luxemburgist_satisfaction -= 2; lean_ultraleft += 1

The districts elect their delegates in crowded, angry meetings. Whatever the congress decides, nobody will be able to say it was not the party's own decision.
